The method is correct for **same-resource + overlapping time**. The worthwhile upgrades are grouping, scanning less work, and mapping a clean DTO.

**1. Group with a `Map`, then conflict-check inside each resource**

`result.find(...)` on every slot is extra linear work. You also scan **all** slots for conflicts even though you already require `other.resourceId === slot.resourceId`. Group first, then only compare siblings:

```ts
const slotsByResource = new Map<number, Slot[]>();
for (const slot of slots) {
  const group = slotsByResource.get(slot.resourceId) ?? [];
  group.push(slot);
  slotsByResource.set(slot.resourceId, group);
}

return [...slotsByResource.entries()].map(([resourceId, resourceSlots]) => ({
  resourceId,
  slots: resourceSlots.map((slot) => ({
    ...toSlotDto(slot),
    conflicts: resourceSlots
      .filter((other) => other.id !== slot.id && slotsOverlap(slot, other))
      .map(toSlotDto),
  })),
}));
```

**2. Extract `slotsOverlap` and `toSlotDto`**

You will need both in `addSlot` and `updateSlot`. `{ ...slot, conflicts }` can leak TypeORM fields; map `id`, `name`, `start`, `end`, `resourceId` explicitly.

**3. Assignment gaps (only if you want the spec, not this rule)**

This method still does not: emit one entry per resource (Lane 4 has no own slots), attach blocker slots (Pool on Lane 1), or treat Pool→Lane as a conflict. You rolled that back on purpose; leave it until you want blocking dependencies.

The overlap scan can stay O(n²) per resource. With this seed size, an interval tree is not worth it.

